      {
        "heading": "The data pipeline is most of the work",
        "bullets": [
          "<b>Collection</b>: Common Crawl and its cleaned derivatives, plus books, code, Wikipedia, papers and curated forums. Raw web text alone produces a noticeably worse model than the same volume of mixed, filtered text.",
          "<b>Filtering</b> removes boilerplate, navigation, gibberish and machine translation, usually with a mix of cheap heuristics and a small quality classifier. Most of Common Crawl is discarded — often over 90%.",
          "<b>Deduplication</b> is the highest-value step and the most underrated. Near-duplicate documents cause memorization rather than learning, and removing them improves quality <i>and</i> shortens training.",
          "<b>Mixing and contamination</b>: proportions are chosen deliberately (code improves reasoning even for non-code tasks), and benchmark text must be removed from training or your evaluation measures recall, not ability."
        ],
        "narration_en": "If you take one thing from this lesson, take this: at scale, data work is not preparation for the real project. It is the real project. Teams that report the largest quality gains almost always got them from the corpus rather than from the architecture, and the architecture has barely changed in years. Four stages. Collection. The bulk of the text comes from Common Crawl, an open snapshot of a large fraction of the public web, or from cleaned derivatives of it. But raw web text alone makes a noticeably worse model than a mixture, so labs add books, source code, Wikipedia, academic papers, and selected forums and discussion sites. The reason mixture helps is that these sources fail in different ways: the web is broad and shallow and full of junk, books are long-form and coherent but old and narrow, code is precisely structured, papers are dense and correct. Together they cover things none of them covers alone. Filtering. Most of what you collect is not text you want a model to imitate. Navigation menus, cookie banners, SEO word salad, auto-generated listings, low-quality machine translation, and pages that are almost entirely markup. The standard approach layers cheap heuristics — document length, punctuation ratios, fraction of lines ending in a full stop, presence of banned tokens — with a small learned classifier trained to recognize text that looks like a reference corpus. The proportion discarded is startling to people who have not seen it: over ninety percent of raw Common Crawl typically does not survive. Deduplication, and this is the step that is both the most valuable and the most often skipped in smaller efforts. The web is enormously redundant — the same article appears on hundreds of sites, the same licence text on millions of pages, the same boilerplate in every template. If a passage appears five thousand times, the model does not learn the general pattern behind it, it memorizes the passage. Exact deduplication is easy; near-duplicate detection, usually with MinHash, is what actually matters. Removing near-duplicates improves quality and shortens training, which is a rare combination and the reason it is the first thing to do. Then mixing, which is a set of deliberate proportion choices rather than a default. One finding worth knowing because it is counter-intuitive: including a substantial fraction of source code improves performance on reasoning tasks that have nothing to do with programming, apparently because code is text with rigorous, checkable structure. And finally contamination, which is a discipline rather than a stage. If your benchmark's test questions are somewhere in your training data — and on a web-scale corpus they very likely are, because benchmarks get posted and discussed — then your evaluation is measuring recall rather than ability. Decontamination means actively searching the corpus for your evaluation sets and removing them, and a model card that does not mention doing it has told you something about how much to trust its numbers.",
        "narration_ne": "यो पाठबाट एउटा कुरा लैजाने हो भने, यो लैजाऊ: मापनमा, data को काम साँचो परियोजनाको तयारी होइन। त्यही साँचो परियोजना हो। सबैभन्दा ठूलो गुणस्तर वृद्धि रिपोर्ट गर्ने टोलीले लगभग सधैं architecture बाट होइन corpus बाट पाएका हुन्छन्, र architecture वर्षौंदेखि लगभग फेरिएको छैन। चार चरण। सङ्कलन। अधिकांश text Common Crawl बाट आउँछ, सार्वजनिक web को ठूलो अंशको खुला snapshot, वा त्यसका सफा गरिएका रूपबाट। तर खाली web text ले मिश्रणभन्दा स्पष्ट रूपमा नराम्रो model बनाउँछ, त्यसैले प्रयोगशालाले किताब, source code, Wikipedia, शैक्षिक पत्र, र छानिएका forum र छलफल साइट थप्छन्। मिश्रणले मद्दत गर्नुको कारण यी स्रोत फरक तरिकाले असफल हुन्छन्: web फराकिलो र छिपछिपे र फोहोरले भरिएको छ, किताब लामो र सुसङ्गत छन् तर पुराना र साँघुरा, code ठीकसँग संरचित छ, पत्र घना र सही छन्। सँगै तिनले त्यस्ता कुरा ढाक्छन् जुन कुनैले एक्लै ढाक्दैन। छानाइ। तिमीले जम्मा गरेको अधिकांश कुरा तिमी model ले नक्कल गरोस् भन्ने चाहने text होइन। navigation menu, cookie banner, SEO को शब्द सलाद, स्वतः बनेका सूची, कम गुणस्तरको मेसिन अनुवाद, र लगभग पूरै markup भएका पृष्ठ। मानक तरिकाले सस्ता heuristic — कागजातको लम्बाइ, विरामचिन्हको अनुपात, पूर्णविरामले सकिने हरफको अंश, प्रतिबन्धित token को उपस्थिति — लाई सन्दर्भ corpus जस्तो देखिने text चिन्न तालिम पाएको सानो सिकिएको classifier सँग तह लगाउँछ। फालिने अनुपात नदेखेकालाई चकित पार्ने खालको छ: खाली Common Crawl को नब्बे प्रतिशतभन्दा बढी सामान्यतया बाँच्दैन। Deduplication, र यो पाइला सबैभन्दा मूल्यवान् र साना प्रयासमा सबैभन्दा बढी छोडिने दुवै हो। web अत्यन्त दोहोरिने छ — उही लेख सयौं साइटमा देखिन्छ, उही अनुमतिपत्रको पाठ लाखौं पृष्ठमा, उही ढाँचा हरेक template मा। कुनै अनुच्छेद पाँच हजार पटक देखिन्छ भने, model ले त्यसपछाडिको सामान्य ढाँचा सिक्दैन, त्यो अनुच्छेद कण्ठ पार्छ। ठ्याक्कै दोहोरिएका हटाउनु सजिलो छ; लगभग-दोहोरिएका पत्ता लगाउनु, प्रायः MinHash ले, साँच्चै महत्त्वपूर्ण कुरा हो। लगभग-दोहोरिएका हटाउँदा गुणस्तर सुध्रन्छ र तालिम छोटो हुन्छ, जुन दुर्लभ संयोजन हो र त्यसैले यो पहिले गर्ने कुरा हो। अनि मिश्रण, जुन पूर्वनिर्धारित होइन जानाजान गरिने अनुपातका छनोट हुन्। जान्न लायक एउटा निष्कर्ष किनभने यो अन्तर्ज्ञानविपरीत छ: source code को ठूलो अंश समावेश गर्दा programming सँग कुनै सम्बन्ध नभएका तर्क कार्यमा प्रदर्शन सुध्रन्छ, देखिन्छ किनभने code कठोर, जाँच्न सकिने संरचना भएको text हो। र अन्त्यमा contamination, जुन चरण होइन अनुशासन हो। तिम्रो मापदण्डका परीक्षा प्रश्न तिम्रो training data मा कतै छन् भने — र web-मापनको corpus मा तिनी धेरै सम्भावित छन्, किनभने मापदण्ड पोस्ट र छलफल हुन्छन् — तिम्रो मूल्याङ्कनले क्षमता होइन सम्झना नापिरहेको छ। Decontamination भनेको corpus मा आफ्ना मूल्याङ्कन सेट सक्रिय रूपमा खोजेर हटाउनु हो, र त्यो गरेको नबताउने model card ले तिमीलाई त्यसका number कति पत्याउने भन्नेबारे केही भनिसक्यो।"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Scaling laws: what Chinchilla actually found",
        "bullets": [
          "Loss falls as a smooth <b>power law</b> in parameters, data and compute. Smooth enough that you can fit the curve on small runs and predict, before spending anything, roughly what a large run will achieve.",
          "The 2020 reading of those curves said: given more compute, mostly make the model bigger. The industry followed it, and GPT-3 at 175B was trained on 300B tokens.",
          "<b>Chinchilla (2022) redid the experiment and found the ratio was wrong.</b> Parameters and tokens should scale together — roughly <b>20 tokens per parameter</b>. A 70B model trained on 1.4T tokens beat the 175B one.",
          "The correction has a sharp practical edge: <b>inference cost scales with parameters, not with training tokens.</b> A smaller model trained longer is cheaper forever after, which is why 7B–70B models are now trained far past the compute-optimal point."
        ],
        "narration_en": "This is one of the few places in deep learning with a genuinely predictive quantitative theory, and it is worth understanding because it drove billions of dollars of decisions, including one large mistake. The empirical finding is this: test loss decreases as a smooth power law in three quantities — the number of parameters, the number of training tokens, and the total compute. Plot loss against any of them on log axes and you get a remarkably straight line, over many orders of magnitude, with no bumps and no plateau in the range anyone has measured. That smoothness is the useful part. It means you can run a series of small, cheap experiments, fit the curve, and extrapolate to predict what a run a thousand times larger will achieve — before you spend the money. Very little else in this field lets you do that. Now the mistake, because it is instructive. In twenty twenty, the Kaplan scaling laws were read as saying that when you get more compute, you should spend most of it on making the model bigger, and comparatively little on more data. The industry took that seriously, and it is why GPT-3 has a hundred and seventy-five billion parameters and was trained on only three hundred billion tokens. That is under two tokens per parameter. In twenty twenty-two, DeepMind redid the experiment more carefully — over four hundred training runs, sweeping model size and token count against a fixed compute budget — and found the earlier conclusion was wrong. The optimal allocation is to scale parameters and tokens roughly together, at about twenty tokens per parameter. They demonstrated it directly: Chinchilla, at seventy billion parameters trained on one point four trillion tokens, using the same compute as the much larger Gopher, beat it on essentially every benchmark. Most of the models trained in the preceding two years were substantially undertrained for their size. Now the twist that makes this more than a historical correction, and it is the part that actually shapes what you can run today. Compute-optimal means optimal for the training run. But a model is trained once and then serves inference for years, and inference cost is set by the number of parameters — not by how many tokens it was trained on. Training a smaller model for much longer costs more up front and then is cheaper on every single call, forever. So once you account for deployment, the right thing to do is deliberately overshoot Chinchilla: take a seven-billion or seventy-billion parameter model and train it on far more tokens than compute-optimality would suggest. That is precisely what Llama and the open-weight families that followed did — trained well past the optimal point on purpose — and it is the direct reason you can run a genuinely capable model on one consumer GPU. The scaling law told the field what was efficient to train. Economics told it what was worth deploying, and the two answers are different.",
        "narration_ne": "deep learning मा साँच्चै भविष्यसूचक परिमाणात्मक सिद्धान्त भएका थोरै ठाउँमध्ये यो एक हो, र बुझ्न लायक छ किनभने यसले अर्बौं डलरका निर्णय चलायो, एउटा ठूलो गल्तीसहित। अनुभवजन्य निष्कर्ष यो हो: परीक्षा loss तीन परिमाणमा सम्म power law अनुसार घट्छ — parameter को सङ्ख्या, training token को सङ्ख्या, र कुल compute। तीमध्ये कुनैविरुद्ध loss लाई log अक्षमा कोर अनि तिमी उल्लेखनीय रूपमा सीधा रेखा पाउँछौ, धेरै परिमाणको क्रममा, कुनै उकाली-ओराली र कसैले नापेको दायरामा कुनै पठारबिना। त्यो सम्मपन नै उपयोगी भाग हो। अर्थात् तिमी साना, सस्ता प्रयोगको शृङ्खला चलाएर, curve मिलाएर, हजार गुणा ठूलो run ले के हासिल गर्ला भविष्यवाणी गर्न सक्छौ — पैसा खर्चिनुअघि। यो क्षेत्रमा अरू थोरैले मात्र त्यो गर्न दिन्छ। अब गल्ती, किनभने त्यो शिक्षाप्रद छ। दुई हजार बीसमा, Kaplan का scaling law यसरी पढिए कि तिमीले बढी compute पाउँदा, अधिकांश model ठूलो बनाउनमा खर्चिनुपर्छ, र तुलनात्मक रूपमा थोरै बढी data मा। उद्योगले त्यसलाई गम्भीरतापूर्वक लियो, र त्यसैले GPT-3 मा एक सय पचहत्तर अर्ब parameter छन् र त्यो केवल तीन सय अर्ब token मा तालिम पाएको थियो। त्यो प्रति parameter दुई token भन्दा कम हो। दुई हजार बाइसमा, DeepMind ले प्रयोग बढी होसियारीसाथ दोहोर्‍यायो — चार सयभन्दा बढी तालिम run, निश्चित compute बजेटविरुद्ध model आकार र token सङ्ख्या घुमाउँदै — र अघिल्लो निष्कर्ष गलत थियो भेट्टायो। इष्टतम बाँडफाँड parameter र token लाई लगभग सँगै मापन गर्नु हो, प्रति parameter लगभग बीस token मा। तिनले सिधै देखाए: Chinchilla, सत्तरी अर्ब parameter मा एक दशमलव चार खर्ब token मा तालिम पाएको, धेरै ठूलो Gopher कै compute प्रयोग गरेर, लगभग हरेक मापदण्डमा त्यसलाई जित्यो। अघिल्ला दुई वर्षमा तालिम पाएका अधिकांश model आफ्नो आकारका लागि निकै कम तालिमप्राप्त थिए। अब त्यो मोड जसले यसलाई ऐतिहासिक सुधारभन्दा बढी बनाउँछ, र आज तिमी के चलाउन सक्छौ त्यो त्यसैले तय गर्छ। Compute-optimal को अर्थ तालिम run का लागि इष्टतम हो। तर model एक पटक तालिम पाउँछ र अनि वर्षौं inference दिन्छ, र inference को लागत parameter को सङ्ख्याले तय हुन्छ — कति token मा तालिम पायो त्यसले होइन। सानो model लाई धेरै लामो तालिम दिँदा सुरुमा बढी खर्च हुन्छ र अनि हरेक call मा, सधैं, सस्तो हुन्छ। त्यसैले deployment हिसाब गर्दा, सही काम जानाजान Chinchilla नाघ्नु हो: सात अर्ब वा सत्तरी अर्ब parameter को model लेऊ र compute-optimality ले सुझाउनेभन्दा धेरै बढी token मा तालिम देऊ। Llama र त्यसपछिका open-weight परिवारले ठ्याक्कै त्यही गरे — जानाजान इष्टतम बिन्दु नाघेर तालिम दिए — र त्यही सिधा कारणले तिमी साँच्चै सक्षम model एउटा उपभोक्ता GPU मा चलाउन सक्छौ। scaling law ले क्षेत्रलाई तालिम दिन के कुशल छ भन्यो। अर्थशास्त्रले पठाउन के लायक छ भन्यो, र दुई जवाफ फरक छन्।"
      },
      {
        "heading": "The Grand Fleet: one cargo too big for any hull",
        "bullets": [
          "Seven crews sail under one flag. Some carry the same kind of cargo to different ports — <b>data parallelism</b>: every ship has a full copy of the plan and a different share of the work, and they reconcile at each rendezvous.",
          "Some cargo will not fit in any single hull, so one load is split across several ships that must arrive together. <b>Model parallelism</b> — and now no ship is useful alone.",
          "The fleet only works if the ships stay in step. One slow ship holds up every rendezvous, and one lost ship costs the whole leg unless there is a checkpoint to fall back to.",
          "And provisioning decides the voyage as much as the ships do. Rotten barrels are worse than empty ones, and loading the same crate five thousand times fills the hold without feeding anyone."
        ],
        "narration_en": "The Grand Fleet is the right picture for this, because a fleet is not just more ships — it is a coordination problem, and coordination is what distributed training actually is. Start with the simple arrangement. Seven crews sail under one flag, and for most of the voyage each ship is doing the same kind of work on a different share of it. Every captain has the full plan. Every ship carries its own portion of the cargo to its own set of ports, and then they meet at a rendezvous point, compare notes, agree on what the plan should now be, and sail on with the updated plan. That is data parallelism, and it is the common case. Every accelerator holds a complete copy of the model, each one processes a different slice of the batch, and at the end of every step they exchange gradients and average them so that all copies stay identical. The rendezvous is the all-reduce, and it is where the network cost lives. Now the harder case. Some cargo will not fit in any single hull, no matter which ship you pick. So one load is cut into parts and distributed across several ships that must now arrive at the same place at the same time, because the cargo is only useful reassembled. That is model parallelism, and it changes the character of the fleet completely: no individual ship is carrying anything usable on its own. In practice this comes in two flavours. Splitting a single large matrix across devices is tensor parallelism, and it needs communication inside every layer, so those ships must be lashed together with the fastest links you have. Putting different layers on different devices is pipeline parallelism, and it needs communication only at the handover points, so those ships can be further apart — but now there is a new problem, which is that ship four has nothing to do until ship three passes something along, and keeping the pipeline full is its own discipline. Two properties of fleets, and both are properties of distributed training. First, the fleet moves at the speed of its slowest ship, because everyone waits at the rendezvous. One straggler — a slower accelerator, a congested link, a machine doing something else — sets the pace for thousands of others, and this is why real training runs spend so much effort on load balance. Second, a fleet loses ships. On a run of thousands of machines for months, hardware failure is not a risk, it is a certainty, and it happens repeatedly. That is why checkpointing is not optional: without a recent saved state, one dead node costs you everything since the last one. And finally the provisions, because it is the same lesson as the data slide and worth hearing twice. A voyage is decided as much by what was loaded as by how well the ships sail. Rotten barrels are worse than empty ones, because the crew eats them and gets sick. And loading the same crate five thousand times fills the hold, looks like an enormous supply, and feeds nobody.",
        "narration_ne": "यसका लागि Grand Fleet ठीक चित्र हो, किनभने बेडा भनेको बढी जहाज मात्र होइन — त्यो समन्वयको समस्या हो, र वितरित तालिम साँच्चै त्यही हो। सरल व्यवस्थाबाट सुरु गर। सात crew एउटै झन्डामुनि यात्रा गर्छन्, र यात्राको अधिकांशमा हरेक जहाजले उही किसिमको काम त्यसको फरक अंशमा गर्दैछ। हरेक कप्तानसँग पूरै योजना छ। हरेक जहाजले आफ्नो भारीको अंश आफ्नै बन्दरगाहसम्म बोक्छ, र अनि तिनी भेटघाट बिन्दुमा भेट्छन्, कुरा मिलाउँछन्, अब योजना के हुनुपर्छ सहमत हुन्छन्, र अद्यावधिक योजनासहित अघि बढ्छन्। त्यो data parallelism हो, र त्यो सामान्य अवस्था हो। हरेक accelerator सँग model को पूरै प्रति हुन्छ, हरेकले batch को फरक अंश प्रशोधन गर्छ, र हरेक पाइलाको अन्त्यमा तिनले gradient साटासाट गरेर औसत निकाल्छन् ताकि सबै प्रति उही रहून्। भेटघाट all-reduce हो, र network को लागत त्यहीँ बस्छ। अब गाह्रो अवस्था। कुनै भारी कुनै पनि एउटै जहाजमा अट्दैन, जुनसुकै जहाज छाने पनि। त्यसैले एउटा भारी टुक्रामा काटेर धेरै जहाजमा बाँडिन्छ जुन अब एउटै ठाउँमा एउटै समयमा पुग्नैपर्छ, किनभने भारी फेरि जोडिएर मात्र उपयोगी हुन्छ। त्यो model parallelism हो, र यसले बेडाको चरित्र पूरै फेर्छ: कुनै पनि एउटा जहाजले आफैंमा प्रयोगयोग्य कुरा बोकेको हुँदैन। व्यवहारमा यो दुई किसिमको हुन्छ। एउटा ठूलो matrix लाई यन्त्रभरि बाँड्नु tensor parallelism हो, र यसलाई हरेक layer भित्र सञ्चार चाहिन्छ, त्यसैले ती जहाज तिमीसँग भएको सबैभन्दा छिटो डोरीले बाँधिनुपर्छ। फरक layer फरक यन्त्रमा राख्नु pipeline parallelism हो, र यसलाई हस्तान्तरण बिन्दुमा मात्र सञ्चार चाहिन्छ, त्यसैले ती जहाज टाढा हुन सक्छन् — तर अब नयाँ समस्या छ, जुन हो जहाज तीनले केही नपठाएसम्म जहाज चारसँग गर्ने काम छैन, र pipeline भरिराख्नु आफ्नै अनुशासन हो। बेडाका दुई गुण, र दुवै वितरित तालिमका गुण हुन्। पहिलो, बेडा आफ्नो सबैभन्दा सुस्त जहाजको गतिमा हिँड्छ, किनभने सबै भेटघाटमा कुर्छन्। एउटा पछौटे — सुस्त accelerator, भीडभाड भएको लिंक, अरू केही गरिरहेको मेसिन — ले हजारौंको गति तय गर्छ, र त्यसैले साँचा तालिम run ले load सन्तुलनमा यति मिहिनेत खर्चिन्छन्। दोस्रो, बेडाले जहाज गुमाउँछ। महिनौंका लागि हजारौं मेसिनको run मा, hardware असफलता जोखिम होइन, निश्चितता हो, र यो बारम्बार हुन्छ। त्यसैले checkpointing वैकल्पिक होइन: भर्खरको बचाइएको state बिना, एउटा मरेको node ले तिमीलाई अघिल्लोपछिको सबै खर्चाउँछ। र अन्त्यमा रसद, किनभने यो data slide कै पाठ हो र दुई पटक सुन्न लायक छ। यात्रा जहाज कति राम्रो चल्छन् भन्नेजत्तिकै के लोड गरियो भन्नेले तय गर्छ। कुहिएका ब्यारेल खालीभन्दा नराम्रा हुन्छन्, किनभने crew ले ती खान्छ र बिरामी पर्छ। र उही बाकस पाँच हजार पटक लोड गर्दा भण्डार भरिन्छ, विशाल आपूर्तिजस्तो देखिन्छ, र कसैलाई खुवाउँदैन।"
      },
      {
        "heading": "What you get at the end: a base model, not an assistant",
        "bullets": [
          "<b>Mixed precision</b> is how any of this fits: run the matrix multiplies in 16-bit (bf16 is preferred — same exponent range as fp32, so no loss-scaling gymnastics) while keeping a 32-bit master copy of the weights.",
          "Add <b>gradient checkpointing</b> — recompute activations in the backward pass instead of storing them — and you trade about 30% more compute for a large memory saving. These two tricks decide what fits.",
          "What emerges is a <b>base model</b>: a very good next-token predictor with no notion of being asked something. Give it a question and a plausible continuation is a list of similar questions, because that is what the web looks like.",
          "Turning that into an assistant is <b>a separate, much cheaper stage</b> — instruction tuning and preference alignment, which are the next two lessons. Pretraining buys capability; alignment buys behaviour."
        ],
        "narration_en": "Two engineering techniques before the punchline, because they are the ones that decide what is possible on the hardware you have. Mixed precision. Thirty-two-bit floats are more precision than neural network arithmetic needs, and they cost you memory and bandwidth for nothing. So you run the matrix multiplications in sixteen bits while keeping a thirty-two-bit master copy of the weights for the optimizer update, where the small increments genuinely do need the precision. There are two sixteen-bit formats and the difference matters. Standard half precision has a narrow exponent range, so small gradients underflow to zero and you need loss scaling — multiplying the loss by a large constant before the backward pass and dividing it out afterwards — to keep them representable. Brain float sixteen instead keeps the same exponent range as thirty-two-bit and gives up mantissa bits, which for gradients is the right trade, and it removes the loss-scaling machinery entirely. Every modern accelerator supports it and it is the default now. Second, gradient checkpointing. During the forward pass you normally store every activation because backward needs them, and on a deep model those activations are most of your memory. Instead, store only a few, and recompute the rest during the backward pass from the nearest stored point. You pay roughly thirty percent more compute and you get a large fraction of your memory back, which very often is the difference between a batch size that trains and one that does not fit. Now the punchline, and it is the most important sentence in the lesson. What comes out at the end of all of this — the data pipeline, the fleet, the months of compute — is a base model. It is an extraordinarily good next-token predictor and it is not an assistant. It has no notion of being asked a question, no concept of a helpful answer, no idea that there is a user. Ask a base model what the capital of Nepal is and a perfectly reasonable continuation is another question, and then another, because lists of questions are a thing that exists on the web and your text looked like the start of one. Ask it for advice and you may get a plausible forum thread including the replies. It is not being unhelpful. Helpfulness was never in the objective; the objective was to predict what text follows, and it is doing that correctly. So there is a second stage, and the encouraging thing is that it is very much cheaper than the first. Instruction tuning teaches the format of a request and a response, using a comparatively small set of curated examples. Preference alignment then teaches which of two valid responses a person would rather have. Together they cost a tiny fraction of pretraining and they are the entire difference between a text predictor and something you can talk to. Pretraining buys capability. Alignment buys behaviour. They are separate stages, they fail in separate ways, and the next two lessons are about the second one.",
        "narration_ne": "मुख्य कुराअघि दुई इन्जिनियरिङ प्रविधि, किनभने तिमीसँग भएको hardware मा के सम्भव छ ती नै तय गर्छन्। Mixed precision। बत्तीस-बिट float neural network को अङ्कगणितलाई चाहिनेभन्दा बढी शुद्धता हुन्, र ती तिमीलाई बिनाकारण memory र bandwidth खर्चाउँछन्। त्यसैले तिमी matrix गुणन सोह्र बिटमा चलाउँछौ र optimizer अद्यावधिकका लागि weight को बत्तीस-बिट मुख्य प्रति राख्छौ, जहाँ साना वृद्धिलाई साँच्चै शुद्धता चाहिन्छ। दुई सोह्र-बिट ढाँचा छन् र फरकले अर्थ राख्छ। मानक आधा शुद्धतामा साँघुरो घातांक दायरा हुन्छ, त्यसैले साना gradient शून्यमा झर्छन् र तिमीलाई loss scaling चाहिन्छ — backward pass अघि loss लाई ठूलो स्थिरले गुणन गर्ने र पछि भाग गर्ने — तिनलाई प्रतिनिधित्वयोग्य राख्न। Brain float सोह्रले बरु बत्तीस-बिटकै घातांक दायरा राख्छ र mantissa बिट छोड्छ, जुन gradient का लागि सही सट्टा हो, र यसले loss-scaling को पूरै संयन्त्र हटाउँछ। हरेक आधुनिक accelerator ले यसलाई समर्थन गर्छ र अहिले यही पूर्वनिर्धारित हो। दोस्रो, gradient checkpointing। forward pass मा तिमी सामान्यतया हरेक activation भण्डारण गर्छौ किनभने backward लाई ती चाहिन्छन्, र गहिरो model मा ती activation तिम्रो अधिकांश memory हुन्। बरु, केही मात्र राख, र बाँकीलाई backward pass मा नजिकको राखिएको बिन्दुबाट फेरि गणना गर। तिमी लगभग तीस प्रतिशत बढी compute तिर्छौ र आफ्नो memory को ठूलो अंश फिर्ता पाउँछौ, जुन प्रायः तालिम पाउने batch आकार र नअट्ने batch आकार बीचको फरक हुन्छ। अब मुख्य कुरा, र यो पाठको सबैभन्दा महत्त्वपूर्ण वाक्य हो। यी सबैको अन्त्यमा निस्कने कुरा — data pipeline, बेडा, महिनौंको compute — एउटा base model हो। यो असाधारण राम्रो अर्को-token अनुमानकर्ता हो र यो सहायक होइन। यसलाई प्रश्न सोधिएको छ भन्ने कुनै धारणा छैन, सहयोगी जवाफको कुनै अवधारणा छैन, प्रयोगकर्ता छ भन्ने कुनै विचार छैन। base model लाई नेपालको राजधानी के हो सोध अनि पूर्ण रूपमा उचित निरन्तरता अर्को प्रश्न हो, र अनि अर्को, किनभने प्रश्नका सूची web मा हुने कुरा हो र तिम्रो text त्यस्तै एउटाको सुरुजस्तो देखियो। यसलाई सल्लाह माग अनि तिमी जवाफसहितको विश्वसनीय forum थ्रेड पाउन सक्छौ। यो असहयोगी भइरहेको होइन। सहयोगिता उद्देश्यमा कहिल्यै थिएन; उद्देश्य कुन text पछि आउँछ अनुमान गर्नु थियो, र यसले त्यो सही गर्दैछ। त्यसैले दोस्रो चरण छ, र उत्साहजनक कुरा यो हो कि त्यो पहिलोभन्दा धेरै सस्तो छ। Instruction tuning ले अनुरोध र जवाफको ढाँचा सिकाउँछ, तुलनात्मक रूपमा सानो छानिएका example को समूह प्रयोग गरेर। Preference alignment ले अनि दुई वैध जवाफमध्ये मान्छेले कुन रुचाउँछ सिकाउँछ। सँगै तिनले pretraining को सानो अंश खर्चिन्छन् र text अनुमानकर्ता र तिमीले कुरा गर्न सक्ने कुराबीचको पूरै फरक तिनै हुन्। Pretraining ले क्षमता किन्छ। Alignment ले व्यवहार किन्छ। ती छुट्टै चरण हुन्, ती छुट्टै तरिकाले असफल हुन्छन्, र अर्का दुई पाठ दोस्रोबारे हुन्।"
      }
