  {
    "id": "finetuning-lora",
    "title": "Fine-Tuning and LoRA: Adapting a Model You Cannot Afford to Move",
    "subtitle_en": "You do not reforge Wado Ichimonji; you rewrap the hilt for the fight",
    "subtitle_ne": "तिमी Wado Ichimonji फेरि पिट्दैनौ; लडाइँका लागि बीँड फेरि बेर्छौ",
    "intro_en": "why full fine-tuning stopped being possible, the low-rank trick that made it possible again on one GPU, the recipe that actually works, and the things fine-tuning cannot fix",
    "intro_ne": "पूरै fine-tuning किन असम्भव भयो, त्यो low-rank चालले एउटै GPU मा यसलाई कसरी फेरि सम्भव बनायो, साँच्चै काम गर्ने विधि, र fine-tuning ले ठीक पार्न नसक्ने कुरा",
    "slides": [
      {
        "heading": "Why full fine-tuning stopped being possible",
        "bullets": [
          "The BERT recipe — update every weight on your labelled data — works beautifully at 100M parameters and is simply unaffordable at 7B: about <b>4× the parameters in memory</b> for weights, gradients and Adam's two moments.",
          "A 7B model in bf16 is 14 GB of weights and roughly <b>60–80 GB of training state</b>. That is several data-centre accelerators for a task you wanted to run on one card.",
          "And you get one whole model per task. Ten fine-tunes of a 7B model is 140 GB of near-identical weights, and every one has to be loaded separately to serve.",
          "<b>Catastrophic forgetting</b> is the third cost: train hard on a narrow dataset and general ability degrades, because nothing is protecting the capabilities you did not fine-tune on."
        ],
        "narration_en": "Fine-tuning is not a new idea in this course — BERT gave it to us in Part Five, and the recipe was clean: take a pretrained model, replace the head, update every weight on your labelled examples, and get a specialist for a fraction of the cost of training from scratch. That works beautifully at a hundred million parameters. It stops working, for boring and completely decisive reasons, at seven billion. The reason is the memory arithmetic we did two lessons ago, and it is worth saying out loud because people are surprised by how bad it is. To train a model you need the weights, one gradient per weight, and — with Adam — two moment estimates per weight. Roughly four copies of the model. A seven-billion-parameter model in sixteen-bit floats is fourteen gigabytes of weights, and by the time you have gradients and optimizer state in the mix you are somewhere between sixty and eighty gigabytes before storing a single activation. That is several data-centre accelerators, rented by the hour, for a task you had hoped to run on the one GPU in your machine. And for a seventy-billion model, multiply by ten. The second cost is one people notice later and resent more. Full fine-tuning produces an entire new model per task. If you fine-tune a seven-billion model for ten different jobs, you now own a hundred and forty gigabytes of weights that are, in truth, almost identical to each other and to the original — and each one has to be stored, versioned, shipped and loaded into memory separately to serve. There is no way to have one server hold ten specialists, because each specialist is a whole model. That is an operational problem long before it is a research one. The third cost is catastrophic forgetting, and it is the subtlest. When you update every weight on a narrow dataset, you are optimizing only for that dataset, and nothing in the process is protecting the abilities the model had before. Train hard enough on your support tickets and the model gets better at support tickets and measurably worse at everything else — reasoning, general knowledge, following instructions in a different format. You did not intend to trade those away, and nothing warned you. Now, all three of these problems come from the same source, which is that you are moving every weight. So the obvious question is whether you have to, and the answer — which is the subject of this lesson — turns out to be no, not remotely. You can freeze the entire pretrained model, train something tiny alongside it, and get most of the benefit for about one percent of the cost.",
        "narration_ne": "यो course मा fine-tuning नयाँ विचार होइन — BERT ले हामीलाई भाग पाँचमा दियो, र विधि सफा थियो: pretrained model लेऊ, head साट, आफ्ना लेबल गरिएका example मा हरेक weight अद्यावधिक गर, र शून्यबाट तालिम दिने लागतको अंशमा विशेषज्ञ पाऊ। त्यो दस करोड parameter मा राम्ररी काम गर्छ। यो सात अर्बमा, नीरस र पूर्ण रूपमा निर्णायक कारणले, काम गर्न छाड्छ। कारण दुई पाठअघि गरेको memory को अङ्कगणित हो, र यो कति नराम्रो छ भनेर मानिस अचम्म मान्ने भएकाले ठूलो स्वरमा भन्न लायक छ। model लाई तालिम दिन तिमीलाई weight, प्रति weight एउटा gradient, र — Adam सँग — प्रति weight दुई moment अनुमान चाहिन्छ। मोटामोटी model का चार प्रति। सोह्र-बिट float मा सात अर्ब parameter को model चौध गिगाबाइट weight हो, र gradient र optimizer state मिसिँदा तिमी एउटै activation भण्डारण गर्नुअघि साठीदेखि असी गिगाबाइटको बीचमा हुन्छौ। त्यो घण्टाको भाडामा लिइएका कैयौं data-center accelerator हो, त्यो कामका लागि जुन तिमीले आफ्नो मेसिनको एउटै GPU मा चलाउने आशा गरेका थियौ। र सत्तरी अर्बको model का लागि, दसले गुणन गर। दोस्रो लागत मानिसले पछि ख्याल गर्छन् र बढी रिस उठाउँछन्। पूरै fine-tuning ले प्रति कार्य पूरै नयाँ model बनाउँछ। तिमीले सात अर्बको model लाई दस फरक कामका लागि fine-tune गर्‍यौ भने, अब तिमीसँग एक सय चालीस गिगाबाइट weight छन् जो, साँच्चै भन्दा, एकअर्कासँग र मूलसँग लगभग उस्तै छन् — र हरेकलाई सेवा दिन छुट्टै भण्डारण, संस्करण, पठाउने र memory मा load गर्नुपर्छ। एउटा server ले दस विशेषज्ञ राख्ने उपाय छैन, किनभने हरेक विशेषज्ञ पूरै model हो। त्यो अनुसन्धानको समस्या हुनुभन्दा धेरै पहिले सञ्चालनको समस्या हो। तेस्रो लागत catastrophic forgetting हो, र यो सबैभन्दा सूक्ष्म छ। तिमीले साँघुरो dataset मा हरेक weight अद्यावधिक गर्दा, तिमी त्यही dataset का लागि मात्र अनुकूलन गर्दैछौ, र प्रक्रियाको कुनै कुराले model सँग पहिले भएका क्षमता जोगाइरहेको छैन। आफ्ना support ticket मा पर्याप्त कडा तालिम देऊ अनि model support ticket मा राम्रो हुन्छ र अरू सबैमा नाप्न सकिने गरी नराम्रो — तर्क, सामान्य ज्ञान, फरक ढाँचामा निर्देशन पालना। तिमीले ती साट्ने मनसाय गरेका थिएनौ, र केहीले चेतावनी दिएन। अब, यी तीनै समस्या उही स्रोतबाट आउँछन्, जुन हो तिमी हरेक weight सारिरहेका छौ। त्यसैले स्पष्ट प्रश्न यो हो कि तिमीले सार्नैपर्छ कि पर्दैन, र जवाफ — जुन यो पाठको विषय हो — निस्कन्छ: पर्दैन, कत्ति पनि पर्दैन। तिमी पूरै pretrained model जमाउन सक्छौ, छेउमा सानो कुरा तालिम दिन सक्छौ, र लगभग एक प्रतिशत लागतमा अधिकांश फाइदा पाउन सक्छौ।"
      },
      {
        "heading": "LoRA: learn the update, not the weights",
        "bullets": [
          "Fine-tuning computes <code>W + ΔW</code>. LoRA's observation: that <b>ΔW is low-rank</b> — adaptation moves the weights in a few directions, not in all of them — so factor it as <code>BA</code> with a small inner dimension <i>r</i>.",
          "Freeze <code>W</code>. Train only <code>A</code> (<code>r×d</code>) and <code>B</code> (<code>d×r</code>). For <code>d</code>=4096 and <code>r</code>=8, that is 65K trainable numbers where the original matrix has 16.7M — <b>about 0.4%</b>.",
          "Optimizer state shrinks with the trainable set, so the memory saving is far larger than the parameter count suggests. A 7B fine-tune drops from ~80 GB to something that fits on one consumer card.",
          "<b>The adapter is a separate file of a few megabytes.</b> One base model in memory can serve many adapters, swapped per request — and <code>B</code> is initialized to zero, so training starts as an exact no-op."
        ],
        "narration_en": "Here is the idea, and it is one line of linear algebra with enormous consequences. When you fine-tune, what you end up with is the original weight matrix plus a change: W plus delta-W. The change is the only thing that is task-specific — W was already there. So the question is not how to store a new model; it is how to store delta-W cheaply. And the observation behind LoRA is that delta-W has low intrinsic rank. Adapting a model to a task does not move the weights in all sixteen million directions available to them; it moves them in a small number of directions, and the rest of the matrix is essentially unchanged. If that is true, then delta-W can be factored: instead of one d-by-d matrix, write it as B times A, where A is r by d and B is d by r, and r is small — eight, or sixteen, or sixty-four. The product is still d by d, so it slots into the model unchanged, but you only ever store and train A and B. Put numbers on it, because the numbers are the argument. For a model dimension of four thousand and ninety-six, the original matrix has about sixteen point seven million entries. With r equal to eight, A and B together have sixty-five thousand. That is about four tenths of one percent of the parameters, trainable, and the other ninety-nine point six percent is frozen. Frozen means no gradient, which means no optimizer state, and remember that optimizer state was three of the four copies in our memory calculation. So the saving is much larger than the parameter count alone suggests: a seven-billion fine-tune goes from something needing eighty gigabytes and multiple accelerators to something that fits comfortably on a single consumer graphics card. Two practical consequences that matter as much as the memory. First, what you save at the end is not a model — it is the adapter, A and B, a file of a few megabytes. The base model is unchanged and shared. So you can hold one copy of a seven-billion-parameter model in memory and serve twenty different specializations from it by swapping which adapter is applied, per request. Ten fine-tunes now cost you fourteen gigabytes plus a few tens of megabytes, instead of a hundred and forty gigabytes. That single property is why LoRA took over. Second, a small design detail that is doing real work: B is initialized to zeros while A is initialized randomly. So at the very start of training, B times A is exactly zero, the adapted model is bit-for-bit the original model, and training begins from a known-good state and departs from it gradually. Without that, you would start by injecting random noise into a carefully pretrained model, which is a bad way to begin. And because the change is additive and separate, it is completely reversible — you delete the adapter and you have the original back, which you cannot say for a full fine-tune.",
        "narration_ne": "यहाँ विचार छ, र यो एक हरफको रेखीय बीजगणित हो जसका परिणाम विशाल छन्। तिमीले fine-tune गर्दा, अन्त्यमा तिमीसँग हुने कुरा मूल weight matrix जोड एउटा परिवर्तन हो: W जोड delta-W। परिवर्तन मात्र कार्य-विशिष्ट कुरा हो — W त पहिल्यै थियो। त्यसैले प्रश्न नयाँ model कसरी भण्डारण गर्ने भन्ने होइन; delta-W सस्तोमा कसरी भण्डारण गर्ने भन्ने हो। र LoRA पछाडिको अवलोकन यो हो कि delta-W को आन्तरिक rank कम छ। model लाई कार्यमा अनुकूल बनाउँदा weight लाई उपलब्ध सबै एक करोड सत्सट्ठी लाख दिशामा सारिँदैन; तिनलाई थोरै दिशामा सारिन्छ, र matrix को बाँकी मूलतः नफेरिएको रहन्छ। त्यो सत्य हो भने, delta-W लाई खण्डित गर्न सकिन्छ: एउटा d गुणा d matrix को सट्टा, यसलाई B गुणा A लेख, जहाँ A r गुणा d छ र B d गुणा r छ, र r सानो छ — आठ, वा सोह्र, वा चौंसठ्ठी। गुणनफल अझै d गुणा d हो, त्यसैले यो model मा नफेरी बस्छ, तर तिमी A र B मात्र भण्डारण र तालिम दिन्छौ। यसमा number राख, किनभने number नै तर्क हुन्। चार हजार छयानब्बेको model आयामका लागि, मूल matrix मा लगभग एक करोड सतसट्ठी लाख प्रविष्टि छन्। r बराबर आठ हुँदा, A र B सँगै पैंसठ्ठी हजार छन्। त्यो parameter को लगभग शून्य दशमलव चार प्रतिशत हो, तालिमयोग्य, र बाँकी उनान्सय दशमलव छ प्रतिशत जमेको छ। जमेको भनेको gradient छैन, अर्थात् optimizer state छैन, र सम्झ optimizer state हाम्रो memory गणनाका चार प्रतिमध्ये तीन थियो। त्यसैले बचत parameter सङ्ख्याले सुझाउनेभन्दा धेरै ठूलो छ: सात अर्बको fine-tune असी गिगाबाइट र कैयौं accelerator चाहिने कुराबाट एउटै उपभोक्ता graphics card मा सजिलै अट्ने कुरामा झर्छ। memory जत्तिकै महत्त्वपूर्ण दुई व्यावहारिक परिणाम। पहिलो, अन्त्यमा तिमीले बचाउने कुरा model होइन — त्यो adapter हो, A र B, केही मेगाबाइटको file। base model नफेरिएको र साझा छ। त्यसैले तिमी सात अर्ब parameter को model को एउटा प्रति memory मा राखेर, प्रति अनुरोध कुन adapter लगाउने साटेर, त्यसैबाट बीस फरक विशेषज्ञता दिन सक्छौ। दस fine-tune ले अब तिमीलाई एक सय चालीस गिगाबाइटको सट्टा चौध गिगाबाइट जोड केही दस मेगाबाइट खर्च गराउँछ। त्यही एउटा गुणले LoRA छायो। दोस्रो, सानो डिजाइन विवरण जसले साँचो काम गर्दैछ: B शून्यमा initialize हुन्छ जबकि A अनियमित रूपमा। त्यसैले तालिमको सुरुमै, B गुणा A ठ्याक्कै शून्य हुन्छ, अनुकूलित model बिट-बिट मूल model हुन्छ, र तालिम थाहा भएको राम्रो अवस्थाबाट सुरु हुन्छ र त्यहाँबाट क्रमशः टाढिन्छ। त्यसबिना, तिमी होसियारीसाथ pretrain गरिएको model मा अनियमित हल्ला हालेर सुरु गर्थ्यौ, जुन सुरु गर्ने नराम्रो तरिका हो। र परिवर्तन जोडात्मक र छुट्टै भएकाले, यो पूर्ण रूपमा उल्टाउन सकिन्छ — तिमी adapter मेट्छौ र मूल फिर्ता पाउँछौ, जुन पूरै fine-tune बारे तिमी भन्न सक्दैनौ।"
      },
      {
        "heading": "QLoRA, and the settings that actually matter",
        "bullets": [
          "<b>QLoRA</b> goes further: quantize the frozen base to 4-bit and train the adapter in 16-bit on top of it. The base is never updated, so its precision only has to be good enough to read — which puts a 70B fine-tune on one 48 GB card.",
          "<b>Rank</b>: 8–16 covers most style and format adaptation; 64+ only when teaching genuinely new behaviour. Bigger <i>r</i> is not safer — it mostly buys you a faster route to overfitting.",
          "<b>Which matrices</b>: the original paper adapted only the attention projections; current practice targets all linear layers including the feedforward, which is where two thirds of the weights live and usually helps more.",
          "<b>alpha</b> scales the adapter's contribution by <code>alpha/r</code>. Set <code>alpha = 2r</code> and change <i>r</i> alone — treating them as two independent dials is how people end up tuning the same thing twice."
        ],
        "narration_en": "LoRA solved the optimizer-state problem, and then QLoRA solved what was left, which was that you still had to hold the frozen base model in memory at full precision. The insight is simple once stated: the base model is never updated. Gradients flow through it but nothing is written to it. So its precision only has to be good enough to compute a forward pass and pass a gradient along — it does not need the precision required to accumulate tiny optimizer updates, because it never accumulates anything. So quantize it to four bits. Keep the adapter in sixteen bits, because that is the part that is actually learning. The result is that fine-tuning a seventy-billion-parameter model fits on a single forty-eight-gigabyte card, which two years earlier would have been an absurd claim. There are a few clever details in the paper — a four-bit format designed for the way neural network weights are actually distributed, quantizing the quantization constants, and paging optimizer state to CPU memory when a spike would otherwise crash the run — but the load-bearing idea is the one sentence: a frozen weight needs less precision than a trained one. Now the settings, because there are only four that matter and people waste a lot of time on them. Rank, r, is the size of the bottleneck and the main dial. Eight to sixteen is enough for almost everything people actually want — adopting a tone, following a specific output format, learning a domain vocabulary. Go to sixty-four or higher only when you are teaching genuinely new behaviour rather than adjusting existing behaviour. And resist the instinct that a bigger rank is a safer default: more capacity mostly buys you a faster route to overfitting on the small dataset you are fine-tuning with. Which matrices to adapt. The original paper applied LoRA only to the query and value projections in attention, and that was mostly a demonstration that a very small intervention suffices. Current practice targets every linear layer, including the feedforward — remember that the feedforward holds about two thirds of the model's weights, so leaving it out means leaving most of the model unreachable. Adapting everything at a low rank generally beats adapting a little at a high rank. Alpha, which confuses everyone, is a scaling factor: the adapter's contribution is multiplied by alpha over r. Its purpose is to let you change r without also changing the effective size of the update. The practical advice is to fix alpha at twice r and then treat r as your only dial. People who tune alpha and r independently are usually tuning the same underlying quantity from two directions and getting confused about which one did what. And the last one, briefly: learning rate. LoRA adapters want a much higher learning rate than full fine-tuning — one e minus four rather than two e minus five is a reasonable starting point — because you are training a small number of freshly initialized parameters rather than nudging a large number of already-good ones. Using a full fine-tuning learning rate is the most common reason a LoRA run appears to do nothing.",
        "narration_ne": "LoRA ले optimizer-state को समस्या हल गर्‍यो, र अनि QLoRA ले बाँकी हल गर्‍यो, जुन थियो कि तिमीले अझै जमेको base model पूरै शुद्धतामा memory मा राख्नुपर्थ्यो। अन्तर्दृष्टि भनिसकेपछि सरल छ: base model कहिल्यै अद्यावधिक हुँदैन। gradient यसबाट बग्छन् तर यसमा केही लेखिँदैन। त्यसैले यसको शुद्धता forward pass गणना गर्न र gradient पठाउन पुग्ने मात्र हुनुपर्छ — यसलाई साना optimizer अद्यावधिक जम्मा गर्न चाहिने शुद्धता चाहिँदैन, किनभने यसले केही जम्मा गर्दैन। त्यसैले यसलाई चार बिटमा quantize गर। adapter सोह्र बिटमा राख, किनभने साँच्चै सिकिरहेको भाग त्यही हो। नतिजा यो हो कि सत्तरी अर्ब parameter को model fine-tune गर्नु एउटै अठ्चालीस गिगाबाइट card मा अट्छ, जुन दुई वर्षअघि बेतुक दाबी हुन्थ्यो। पत्रमा केही चतुर विवरण छन् — neural network का weight साँच्चै कसरी वितरित हुन्छन् त्यसका लागि बनाइएको चार-बिट ढाँचा, quantization स्थिरलाई नै quantize गर्नु, र spike ले run crash गर्ने बेला optimizer state CPU memory मा page गर्नु — तर भार बोक्ने विचार त्यो एउटै वाक्य हो: जमेको weight लाई तालिम पाएकोभन्दा कम शुद्धता चाहिन्छ। अब सेटिङ, किनभने महत्त्वपूर्ण चार मात्र छन् र मानिसले तीमा धेरै समय खेर फाल्छन्। Rank, r, अवरोधको आकार र मुख्य गाँठी हो। मानिसले साँच्चै चाहने लगभग सबै कुराका लागि आठदेखि सोह्र पुग्छ — कुनै लय अपनाउनु, खास output ढाँचा पछ्याउनु, कुनै क्षेत्रको शब्दावली सिक्नु। चौंसठ्ठी वा माथि तब मात्र जाऊ जब तिमी भएको व्यवहार मिलाउनुको सट्टा साँच्चै नयाँ व्यवहार सिकाउँदै छौ। र ठूलो rank बढी सुरक्षित पूर्वनिर्धारित हो भन्ने प्रवृत्तिको विरोध गर: बढी क्षमताले प्रायः तिमीले fine-tune गरिरहेको सानो dataset मा overfitting को छिटो बाटो मात्र किन्छ। कुन matrix अनुकूल गर्ने। मूल पत्रले LoRA लाई attention का query र value प्रक्षेपणमा मात्र लगायो, र त्यो प्रायः अत्यन्त सानो हस्तक्षेप पुग्छ भन्ने प्रदर्शन थियो। अहिलेको अभ्यासले हरेक linear layer लाई निशाना बनाउँछ, feedforward सहित — सम्झ feedforward सँग model का लगभग दुई तिहाइ weight छन्, त्यसैले त्यो छोड्नुको अर्थ अधिकांश model पहुँचबाहिर छोड्नु हो। कम rank मा सबै अनुकूल गर्नु उच्च rank मा थोरै अनुकूल गर्नुभन्दा सामान्यतया राम्रो हुन्छ। Alpha, जसले सबैलाई अलमल्याउँछ, मापन गुणक हो: adapter को योगदान alpha भाग r ले गुणन हुन्छ। यसको उद्देश्य तिमीलाई अद्यावधिकको प्रभावकारी आकार नफेरी r फेर्न दिनु हो। व्यावहारिक सल्लाह alpha लाई r को दुई गुणामा स्थिर राख्नु र अनि r लाई मात्र आफ्नो गाँठी मान्नु हो। alpha र r लाई स्वतन्त्र रूपमा मिलाउने मानिस प्रायः उही अन्तर्निहित परिमाणलाई दुई दिशाबाट मिलाउँदै हुन्छन् र कुनले के गर्‍यो भन्नेमा अलमलिन्छन्। र अन्तिम, छोटकरीमा: learning rate। LoRA adapter लाई पूरै fine-tuning भन्दा धेरै अग्लो learning rate चाहिन्छ — दुई e माइनस पाँचको सट्टा एक e माइनस चार उचित सुरुवात हो — किनभने तिमी धेरै पहिल्यै राम्रा parameter घचेट्नुको सट्टा भर्खर initialize गरिएका थोरै parameter तालिम दिँदैछौ। पूरै fine-tuning को learning rate प्रयोग गर्नु LoRA run ले केही नगरेजस्तो देखिनुको सबैभन्दा सामान्य कारण हो।"
      },
      {
        "heading": "The hilt, not the blade",
        "bullets": [
          "Wado Ichimonji is a Great Grade sword that took a master years to forge. Reforging it to suit a particular fight would be absurd — you would spend enormous effort and probably ruin the thing that made it worth having.",
          "So a swordsman does not touch the steel. He changes the wrap, the balance, the grip — small, cheap, reversible adjustments that change how the blade behaves in <i>this</i> hand for <i>this</i> fight.",
          "<b>And he can keep several wraps for one blade</b>, swapped between fights. One sword, many configurations, a few grams each. That is one base model serving many adapters.",
          "But a rewrap cannot make a sword into a spear, and it cannot put an edge on steel that was never hardened. <b>Fine-tuning adjusts how capability is delivered; it does not install capability that is not there.</b>"
        ],
        "narration_en": "Wado Ichimonji is one of the twenty-one Great Grade swords. Somebody spent years forging it, and the properties that make it what it is — the steel, the temper, the geometry of the edge — are settled and are not up for discussion. Now suppose Zoro is facing a specific opponent and wants the blade to behave slightly differently. What he does not do is take it to a forge and melt it down. That would be enormously expensive, it would take a very long time, and the overwhelmingly likely outcome is that he destroys the thing that made the sword worth having in the first place. Nobody reforges a masterwork to suit a Tuesday. What a swordsman actually adjusts is everything around the steel. The wrap on the hilt, for grip in the wet. The balance. The guard. The angle at which it sits at his side. These are small changes, they cost almost nothing, they take an evening, and they genuinely change how the blade performs in this hand for this fight. The steel is untouched. And here is the part that maps most precisely: he can keep several wraps for one blade and swap between them. One sword, several configurations, a few grams each, chosen per opponent. That is LoRA, and I do not think the analogy strains anywhere. The pretrained model is the blade — millions of dollars and months of compute, and its core capabilities are settled. Full fine-tuning is reforging: ruinously expensive at scale, and it risks the general ability that made the model worth using, which is exactly what catastrophic forgetting is. The adapter is the wrap: a few megabytes, an afternoon on one GPU, entirely reversible, and it changes how the model behaves on your task without touching a single pretrained weight. And keeping many adapters for one base model is keeping many wraps for one sword — which is, in production, the whole reason this technique won. But the analogy also tells you the limit, and this is the part to carry into the next slide. A rewrap changes how a sword is wielded. It cannot turn a sword into a spear, and it cannot put a keen edge on steel that was never properly hardened. If the capability is not in the blade, no amount of work on the hilt will produce it. Fine-tuning adjusts how a model's existing capability is delivered — its format, its register, its defaults, its vocabulary. It does not install a capability the base model does not have. People routinely expect otherwise, and it is the most common reason a fine-tuning project disappoints.",
        "narration_ne": "Wado Ichimonji एक्काइस Great Grade तरबारमध्ये एक हो। कसैले यसलाई पिट्न वर्षौं खर्चियो, र यसलाई जे बनाउँछ ती गुण — इस्पात, ताप, धारको ज्यामिति — तय भइसकेका छन् र छलफलका विषय होइनन्। अब मानौं Zoro कुनै खास प्रतिद्वन्द्वीसामु छ र तरबारले अलिकति फरक व्यवहार गरोस् भन्ने चाहन्छ। उसले नगर्ने कुरा हो त्यसलाई भट्टीमा लगेर पगाल्नु। त्यो अत्यन्त महँगो हुन्थ्यो, धेरै लामो समय लाग्थ्यो, र अत्यधिक सम्भावित नतिजा यो हो कि उसले तरबारलाई पहिले नै मूल्यवान् बनाउने कुरा नष्ट गर्छ। कसैले मङ्गलबारका लागि उत्कृष्ट कृति फेरि पिट्दैन। तरबारबाजले साँच्चै मिलाउने कुरा इस्पातवरिपरिको सबै हो। बीँडको बेराइ, भिजेकोमा समात्न। सन्तुलन। रक्षक। कुन कोणमा त्यो उसको छेउमा बस्छ। यी साना परिवर्तन हुन्, यिनले लगभग केही खर्च गर्दैनन्, एक साँझ लाग्छ, र यिनले यो हातमा यो लडाइँका लागि तरबारले कस्तो प्रदर्शन गर्छ साँच्चै फेर्छन्। इस्पात अछुतो छ। र यहाँ त्यो भाग छ जुन सबैभन्दा ठीकसँग मिल्छ: उसले एउटा तरबारका लागि कैयौं बेराइ राख्न र तिनीबीच साट्न सक्छ। एउटा तरबार, कैयौं कन्फिगरेसन, हरेक केही ग्राम, प्रति प्रतिद्वन्द्वी छानिएको। त्यो LoRA हो, र मलाई लाग्दैन उपमा कतै तानिन्छ। pretrained model तरबार हो — लाखौं डलर र महिनौंको compute, र यसका मूल क्षमता तय भइसकेका छन्। पूरै fine-tuning फेरि पिट्नु हो: मापनमा विनाशकारी महँगो, र यसले model लाई प्रयोगयोग्य बनाउने सामान्य क्षमता जोखिममा पार्छ, जुन ठ्याक्कै catastrophic forgetting हो। adapter बेराइ हो: केही मेगाबाइट, एउटा GPU मा एक दिउँसो, पूर्ण रूपमा उल्टाउन सकिने, र यसले एउटै pretrained weight नछोई तिम्रो कार्यमा model को व्यवहार फेर्छ। र एउटा base model का लागि धेरै adapter राख्नु एउटा तरबारका लागि धेरै बेराइ राख्नु हो — जुन, उत्पादनमा, यो प्रविधि जित्नुको पूरै कारण हो। तर उपमाले तिमीलाई सीमा पनि भन्छ, र यही अर्को slide मा लैजाने भाग हो। बेराइले तरबार कसरी चलाइन्छ फेर्छ। यसले तरबारलाई भाला बनाउन सक्दैन, र कहिल्यै ठीकसँग नकठोर पारिएको इस्पातमा धार लगाउन सक्दैन। क्षमता तरबारमै छैन भने, बीँडमा जति काम गरे पनि त्यो बन्दैन। Fine-tuning ले model को भएको क्षमता कसरी दिइन्छ मिलाउँछ — त्यसको ढाँचा, त्यसको लय, त्यसका पूर्वनिर्धारित, त्यसको शब्दावली। यसले base model सँग नभएको क्षमता जडान गर्दैन। मानिसले नियमित रूपमा अर्कै अपेक्षा गर्छन्, र fine-tuning परियोजना निराश पार्नुको सबैभन्दा सामान्य कारण त्यही हो।"
      },
      {
        "heading": "When to fine-tune at all, and what it cannot fix",
        "bullets": [
          "<b>Fine-tune for form.</b> A consistent output format, a house tone, a domain vocabulary, a task done the same way every time, or a small fast model taught to imitate a large slow one. These work reliably.",
          "<b>Do not fine-tune for facts.</b> Knowledge that changes, or that you need cited and auditable, belongs in retrieval — Part 7. Baking a policy document into weights means retraining to change a sentence, with no way to show the source.",
          "The order that saves money: <b>prompt → few-shot → retrieval → fine-tune.</b> Each step costs more and is harder to undo, and most projects that reached for step four should have stopped at step three.",
          "Data beats hyperparameters, decisively. A thousand carefully checked examples in a consistent format outperform fifty thousand scraped ones, and evaluating on a held-out split is the only thing that tells you which you have."
        ],
        "narration_en": "Let us finish with judgement, because the technique is easy now and knowing when not to use it is the valuable part. Fine-tune when you want to change form. If you need output in a specific structure every single time — a particular JSON shape, a report template, a fixed set of fields — fine-tuning is much more reliable than asking politely in a prompt, and it is cheaper per call because you are not spending tokens on instructions. If you need a consistent house tone or persona, fine-tuning gives you that in a way prompting only approximates. If you have a domain with vocabulary and conventions the model handles awkwardly, a few thousand examples fix it. And distillation is a genuinely good use: have a large model produce high-quality outputs, then fine-tune a small fast model to imitate them, and deploy the small one. All of these are adjustments to how existing capability is expressed, which is exactly what the hilt can change. Do not fine-tune to add facts. This is the mistake, and it is made constantly. Knowledge baked into weights is undated, unattributable and unchangeable — to correct a single sentence of a policy document you would have to retrain, and you cannot show a user where an answer came from. Facts that change, facts you need cited, and any corpus you might have to update belong in retrieval, which is the whole of Part Seven. The rule of thumb is: fine-tune behaviour, retrieve knowledge. The escalation order is worth memorizing because it saves real money. Start with a better prompt — it costs an hour and no infrastructure. Then few-shot examples in the prompt, which handle a surprising amount of format specification. Then retrieval, if the gap is knowledge rather than behaviour. Only then fine-tune. Every step up that ladder costs more, takes longer, and is harder to undo, and in my experience most projects that reached step four had a problem that step two or three solves. Fine-tuning is not the sophisticated answer; it is the expensive one, and it is correct only when the cheaper ones have actually been tried and actually failed. And when you do fine-tune, the thing that determines your result is the data, not the hyperparameters. A thousand examples you have personally read, in a consistent format, with the failure cases you care about deliberately represented, will beat fifty thousand scraped ones every time. Garbage examples teach garbage very efficiently. Hold out a test split before you start, look at the model's actual outputs on it rather than just the loss curve, and compare against the base model with a good prompt — because if the base model with a good prompt is close, you have just spent a week to move a number that did not need moving.",
        "narration_ne": "निर्णयले टुङ्ग्याऔं, किनभने प्रविधि अब सजिलो छ र कहिले प्रयोग नगर्ने भन्ने थाहा पाउनु मूल्यवान् भाग हो। रूप फेर्न चाहँदा fine-tune गर। तिमीलाई हरेक पटक खास संरचनामा output चाहिन्छ भने — खास JSON आकार, प्रतिवेदनको template, निश्चित क्षेत्रको समूह — fine-tuning prompt मा नम्रतापूर्वक माग्नुभन्दा धेरै भरपर्दो छ, र प्रति call सस्तो छ किनभने तिमी निर्देशनमा token खर्चिँदैनौ। तिमीलाई निरन्तर घरेलु लय वा व्यक्तित्व चाहिन्छ भने, fine-tuning ले त्यो prompting ले केवल अनुमान गर्ने तरिकाले दिन्छ। model ले अप्ठ्यारो तरिकाले सम्हाल्ने शब्दावली र परिपाटी भएको क्षेत्र छ भने, केही हजार example ले ठीक पार्छ। र distillation साँच्चै राम्रो प्रयोग हो: ठूलो model लाई उच्च गुणस्तरको output बनाउन लगाऊ, अनि सानो छिटो model लाई ती नक्कल गर्न fine-tune गर, र सानो पठाऊ। यी सबै भएको क्षमता कसरी व्यक्त हुन्छ भन्नेमा गरिने मिलाइ हुन्, जुन ठ्याक्कै बीँडले फेर्न सक्ने कुरा हो। तथ्य थप्न fine-tune नगर। यही गल्ती हो, र यो निरन्तर गरिन्छ। weight मा पकाइएको ज्ञान मितिविहीन, स्रोतविहीन र अपरिवर्तनीय हुन्छ — नीति कागजातको एउटा वाक्य सच्याउन तिमीले फेरि तालिम दिनुपर्थ्यो, र जवाफ कहाँबाट आयो प्रयोगकर्तालाई देखाउन सक्दैनौ। फेरिने तथ्य, उद्धरण चाहिने तथ्य, र तिमीले अद्यावधिक गर्नुपर्ने कुनै पनि corpus retrieval मा पर्छन्, जुन पूरै भाग सात हो। अङ्गुठेछाप नियम यो हो: व्यवहार fine-tune गर, ज्ञान retrieve गर। बढ्ने क्रम कण्ठ पार्न लायक छ किनभने यसले साँचो पैसा बचाउँछ। राम्रो prompt बाट सुरु गर — यसले एक घण्टा र कुनै पूर्वाधार खर्च गर्दैन। अनि prompt मा few-shot example, जसले अचम्म लाग्दो मात्रामा ढाँचा तोक्ने काम गर्छ। अनि retrieval, खाडल व्यवहारको होइन ज्ञानको हो भने। त्यसपछि मात्र fine-tune। त्यो भर्‍याङको हरेक पाइलाले बढी खर्च गर्छ, बढी समय लिन्छ, र उल्टाउन गाह्रो हुन्छ, र मेरो अनुभवमा चौथो पाइलामा पुगेका अधिकांश परियोजनाको समस्या दोस्रो वा तेस्रो पाइलाले हल गर्छ। Fine-tuning परिष्कृत जवाफ होइन; महँगो जवाफ हो, र सस्ता जवाफ साँच्चै प्रयास गरिएर साँच्चै असफल भएपछि मात्र सही हो। र तिमीले fine-tune गर्दा, तिम्रो नतिजा तय गर्ने कुरा data हो, hyperparameter होइन। तिमीले आफैं पढेका हजार example, निरन्तर ढाँचामा, तिमीले वास्ता गर्ने असफलताका अवस्था जानाजान समावेश गरेर, हरेक पटक पचास हजार टिपिएका example लाई जित्नेछन्। फोहोर example ले फोहोर धेरै कुशलतापूर्वक सिकाउँछ। सुरु गर्नुअघि परीक्षा अंश छुट्याऊ, त्यसमा loss curve मात्र होइन model को साँचो output हेर, र राम्रो prompt सहितको base model सँग तुलना गर — किनभने राम्रो prompt सहितको base model नजिक छ भने, तिमीले सार्नै नपर्ने number सार्न भर्खर एक हप्ता खर्चियौ।"
      }
    ],
  },

